## Write the message first

Write the message a senior maintainer would want in permanent history. Lead with why the change exists and its observable outcome. Explain non-obvious design decisions, breaking or operational consequences, and only verification actually recorded here: {{ VERIFICATION_FACTS }}. Detail target: **{{ MESSAGE_BUDGET }}**.

Keep a narrow change to one short paragraph. Give a broad change a concise summary followed by a few cohesive paragraphs about behavior, design, migration risk, and verification. Never inventory files or directories, copy the diff, enumerate every edit, or pad the body with generated phrases such as “Changed X.”

## Subject

Write `<kind>(<scope>)?!?: <description>` once. Kind is one of
`feat|fix|docs|refactor|test|style|perf|build|ci|chore`; use `chore` only for repo maintenance, tooling, config, or dependencies. Scope is optional; when absent, omit it and its parentheses. A scope uses only
lowercase letters, digits, `/`, `_`, or `-`: no dots, no uppercase. Use one space after the colon and
a lowercase imperative description. Keep the frontmatter value unquoted. Write the first valid subject;
do not generate alternatives. There is no length limit.

## Body

Omit the body only for a one-line typo, formatting, or comment change; every other commit gets a body. Otherwise
explain why in one `## Body` item. Use `## Body Summary`, `## Body Details`, and
`## Body Footer` instead only for migration steps, breaking changes, or issue
references.

## Document shape

For a commit, use frontmatter `type: commit` and `subject:`, then the selected
body form. When no commit is needed, submit only `type: skip`
and `reason:` frontmatter.

{% set commit_message_example %}
---
type: commit
subject: fix(auth): prevent token expiry race
---

## Body
- [B-1] Prevents a race where refreshed tokens could expire mid-request.
